@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import {
  delegation, brief text, queue place, queued task, status line, result text,     reply budget, session cancel, completion notice, stop notice, collect tool,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  parent adapter, supported child host, entry point, parent host,
}
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task, task id, child run, result, domain event, transcript, tool output, skill content, enqueue time,
    start time, child driver,
}
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, project root, unknown agent error, load error,
  unresolved root error, model endpoint, model tier, routable tier,
      tier error, agent entry, agents map, general agent, agent file,
    parallel limit, SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR,
  fallback agent, tier text, unmatched tier text, agent alias,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  parent session id, event log, task status,
}
@design/claude-routing-hooks.sigil from ClaudeRoutingHooks import {
  Agent tool, CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, pass-back,
}

component ClaudeMcpServer {
  goal {
    Let a Claude Code session or subagent make a delegation through the
    plugin's
    MCP server, with progress while it waits, a way to collect it later, a way
    to cancel it, and an honest report of whether its child has started.
  }

  state {
    The server knows the parent session id of the current conversation. The
    *SessionStart hook* of the plugin records it, so that listing can be
    limited to
    this conversation even after /clear. The hook records one entry for each
    session id together with its own ancestor processes, and the server takes
    the
    newest entry whose ancestors include the Claude Code process that started
    the
    server, so two sessions in one project never take each other's id, and a
    session of another process is never matched. When the server does not yet
    know
    it, or no entry names its own parent process, a delegation carries none.

    A *progress notification* carries a counter that rises with each
    notification
    and one *message line*, and no total. The message line names the agent, the
    kind
    of the latest domain event, the tool name with its target path for tool
    activity, and the elapsed time. While the child run is Pending it says that
    the
    run is waiting, and once the child run is released it no longer says so.

    ClaudeMcpServer is the parent adapter of the Claude Code parent host: the
    entry point of its
    process registers it with Kernel as a parent adapter, and it reaches the
    core
    only through DelegationService, for the delegation, the collect, the
    cancel,
    the session cancel, the completion notice and
    the listing, and through AgentConfig, and through Kernel only to list the
    supported child hosts that it gives AgentConfig, and reports each
    queued task
    that
    DelegationService derives,
    never as started.

    Each reply about a task that has no result gives its status line and each
    reply about a settled task gives its result text, worded by
    DelegationService, so that a queued task of this process names the
    model endpoint, the queue place and the time waited, a queued task of
    another
    process names the model endpoint and the time waited with no queue place,
    and
    a task whose child run has a start time gives that start time. An await
    reply may give a deferred line instead of a result text. ClaudeMcpServer
    adds
    only what is its own: the tool result limit, the deferred line, the
    reply budget it gives each delegation, the listing cap and the
    progress notification.

    The *tool result limit* is Claude Code's inline limit for an MCP tool
    result, 50,000 characters. Claude Code does not truncate a longer text
    result: it saves it to a file in the session's tool-results folder and
    gives Claude its path. The reply budget that the server gives each
    delegation is the tool result limit less a reserve for the first line, the
    notice and the usage line of a result text, 48,000 characters.

    A *deferred line* stands in an await reply for the result of a settled
    task that did not fit: one line with the task id, the agent and the
    task status, saying that the result is not shown here for the reply's size
    and that the tasks tool with that task id reads it whole.

    The *plugin data folder* is the folder that Claude Code gives the plugin
    for
    its own files. It holds the recorded parent session id of each session of
    each project.
  }

  logic {
    When the server receives the resolved project root, it writes a
    *readiness marker*
    named by its own process id in the folder of that project under the
    plugin data folder, holding its process id and the process id of its
    parent, the Claude Code process that started it. On exit it removes the
    readiness marker, which ends that flow.

    On each tool listing, read the agent file again, giving AgentConfig each
    supported child host that Kernel lists, and build the description of
    the delegate tool with each routable tier and the child host and model it
    runs,
    which
    ends that flow. When the load fails, the description falls back to the
    static text, and the tools are still listed.

    On a delegate call that waits, make a delegation with the mapped agent name
    and model tier, the parent session id of the caller and the reply budget.
    When
    the request carries a progress token,
    send a progress notification to the caller for each domain event, at most
    once a second with the latest message line, and after every 30 seconds of
    silence, including while the child run is Pending. A silent heartbeat
    repeats the latest message line with the new elapsed time. Sending the
    progress notification ends that branch. Return the result when the task
    settles, which ends the flow.

    On a delegate call that does not wait, make the delegation the same way and
    return the task id at once with its status line, which ends that flow. A
    later await call waits up to its *await timeout* and then answers for each
    task id
    in the order asked: the status line of a task that has not settled, and for
    a settled task its result text whole while the joined reply stays within
    the
    tool result limit, the first section always, or else its deferred line. The
    flow ends there.

    When the *task mode* is on, a delegate call that does not set wait to true
    is answered with an *MCP task* instead of a task id. The server keeps a
    *MCP task store* backed by DelegationService: creating the MCP task makes the
    delegation without waiting, a status request answers working until the task
    settles, and the completed MCP task gives the completion notice, which
    names
    the await tool as its collect tool, or the
    stop notice for a task that was stopped. A status update to cancelled from
    Claude Code cancels that one task through DelegationService, which ends the
    flow. Each MCP task is kept until its parent session id changes or the
    server
    exits.

    When Claude Code cancels a waited delegate call or a waited await call
    within
    120 seconds of the call, run a session cancel for the current
    parent session id, which ends the flow. A cancel that arrives later, when
    Claude Code has moved the call to the background and only a stop from its
    task list or a timeout can end it, cancels only the task of a
    waited
    delegate
    call and nothing for an await call, which ends the flow.

    On each call, and every 5 seconds while a task of this server is Pending or
    live, read the newest recorded parent session id of its own Claude Code
    process. When it differs from the one the server last read, run a
    session cancel for the one it last read, which ends the flow.

    On a delegate call, map its tier text and its agent name through
    AgentConfig:
    an agent alias names the general agent, an empty tier text keeps the
    agent's
    own tier or the medium tier, and an unmatched tier text or a model tier
    that
    is not configured is returned as the tier error with the pass-back, which
    ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    The server must expose four tools, the delegate tool, the *tasks tool*, the
    await tool and the *cancel tool*, under
    names of the form mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__ followed by the tool, and
    only
    the delegate tool, which starts a delegation, and the await tool must be
    always loaded, and no other tool may be.

    The *server instructions* must carry only the delegation rules. They must
    tell
    the parent model to write a self-contained brief text, because the child
    cannot
    see
    the conversation, and they must not say that a delegation needs a
    configured
    agent definition, because the general agent runs work that names none.

    No tool may cut a result or return a transcript. The delegate tool, the
    tasks tool and the cancel tool return a result text whole, whatever its
    length.

    An await reply must give each result whole or as a deferred line, and
    never a part of one. Its first section is always whole, even when alone it
    is over the tool result limit, and every later result text is whole only
    while the joined reply stays within the tool result limit.

    No reply of the delegate tool, the tasks tool or the await tool may call a
    queued task started or
    running, and every reply about a task whose child run has a start time and
    no result must give its status line with that start time.

    A progress notification must not carry agent message text, tool output,
    tool
    arguments other than a path, the stderr tail or skill content of the
    child run,
    because the event log must never reach the parent model.

    The server must pass the roots that Claude Code reports to the delegation
    service and must never use the working directory of its own process as the
    project root. The plugin sets SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
    for
    it.

    A name other than general that the agents map does not hold must return the
    unknown agent error as a
    *tool error* that names every configured agent entry. When a
    fallback agent is configured, a delegation that names a name other than
    general
    that the agents map does not hold runs the fallback agent, and the reply
    must
    say that
    the named agent is not configured. A load error of a malformed
    agent file must be returned as a tool error as well, with no task made and
    with no pass-back, while a missing agent file is returned as a tool error
    with the pass-back.

    An error of the driver registry for a child host with no registered
    child driver is
    returned like a tier error, and a tier error must be returned as a
    tool error
    that carries the tier error unchanged, with the tier it names, every
    configured
    model tier and, when the
    child host is the cause, that child host and every supported child host,
    and
    says to dispatch
    that subagent through the Agent tool
    instead, as a pass-back whose short description begins with passback, with
    no
    task made.

    The unresolved root error must be returned as a tool error that names
    SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR and says that the configuration must be fixed by the
    user, so that a calling subagent does not retry the call.

    When the server does not know its parent session id, the tasks tool with no
    argument must list every task and say that the conversation could not be
    identified.

    The task mode must be on only while Claude Code advertises MCP task support
    for the call and the session is interactive, because a background result is
    lost when a non-interactive Claude Code exits. The server must write the
    task mode into its readiness marker, so that the routing text and the
    deny reason say how to dispatch for the mode in use. A delegate call that
    sets wait to true must wait in either task mode.

    The cancel tool must accept a call with no task id that runs a
    session cancel
    for the current parent session id, and it must still end a task named by
    its
    task id as cancelled. The result of a task that a session cancel ended must
    be its stop notice, so that Claude is not set working again by it.

    A session cancel run by the server must reach only the tasks of this
    server,
    and a cancel of a task that another process owns must report that and
    change
    nothing.

    A listing from the tasks tool must hold at most 50 tasks, the tasks with a
    start time and no result first, then each queued task, then the rest newest
    first, and it must end with a notice that names how many tasks it left out
    and says that a task can still be read by its task id. This cap is fixed
    and applies whether or not the conversation is known.

    Closing the stdin of the server while a child run is live must leave no
    process
    of that child run behind.

    The server must write its readiness marker only after its project root
    resolves and must remove it on exit, and a readiness marker whose process
    is
    no longer alive must count as absent.

    A plugin cannot ship an *allow rule*. ClaudeMcpServer assumes that the user
    or
    project supplies an allow rule for mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__* before a
    delegation from a subagent. Without it, the delegation raises an approval
    prompt in the main session, and under dontAsk it is denied and the subagent
    sees the denial.

    ClaudeMcpServer depends on DelegationService for the delegation, the
    queue place, the status line and the result text, and on AgentConfig for
    the unknown agent error, the tier error and the routable tier names, and on
    Kernel to list the supported child hosts, and
    on ClaudeRoutingHooks for what the SessionStart hook adds to Claude's
    context
    after
    it records the parent session id, and it must not depend on any particular
    child driver.
  }

  cases {
    In every case here the agent file also holds the medium tier and a
    general agent.

    A delegate call with wait false returns its task id at once, and await with
    a
    short await timeout returns no result for that task while its child run is
    still
    live, only its status line.

    Three delegate calls with wait false on a model endpoint whose
    parallel limit is 1 return the first as running with its start time and
    the second and third as a queued task at place 1 and 2. The tasks tool on
    the second returns its queued status line and never says that its child run
    is running or has started, and after
    the first settles, await on the second returns it running with a
    start time later than its enqueue time.

    A queued task of another process is listed as queued with its time waited
    counted from its enqueue time and no queue place.

    Listing with no argument returns only each task recorded for the current
    parent session id, and returns every task with a notice when the server
    does
    not know it.

    A listing of 60 tasks returns 50, with every task that has a start time and
    no result among them, and a notice that 10 tasks were left out and that
    each can be read by its task id.

    A delegate call with an unknown agent name and no model tier, when no
    fallback agent is configured, returns a tool error that names every
    configured
    agent entry and makes no task. With a fallback agent configured, the same
    call
    runs the fallback agent and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured.

    A delegate call with a model tier that is not configured returns a
    tool error that lists every configured model tier and names the Agent tool,
    and makes no task.

    A tool listing with the model tier medium on r9700/Qwen3.8 shows medium and
    r9700/Qwen3.8 in the delegate description. After the agent file changes,
    the
    next listing shows the new routable tier set, and with a broken agent file
    the tools are still listed with the static description.

    A delegate call that waits for a final message of 20,000 characters
    returns it whole, and the tasks tool and the await tool return it whole.

    An await over six settled tasks whose result texts are about 9,000
    characters each returns the first five whole and a deferred line for the
    sixth, with no cut anywhere, within the tool result limit. An await whose
    first result alone is over the tool result limit returns it whole and gives
    a deferred line for the next settled task.

    The brief of a delegation from the server ends with its reply budget.

    With the model tier small configured, a delegate call with the tier text
    cheapest capable and the agent alias delegate runs the general agent on
    small. In a project with no agent file, a delegate call returns the
    load error with the pass-back and makes no task, and a delegate call with
    no
    tier text runs the general agent on its medium tier even when large is
    configured.

    With the task mode on, two delegate calls that set no wait each return an
    MCP task. When the first settles, its completed MCP task gives the
    completion notice, which names the second task as still running and says to
    wait for it.

    With one waited call of the await tool and two background tasks running, a
    cancel of that call within 120 seconds runs a session cancel that ends both
    tasks as cancelled, and a cancel of the same call after 120 seconds ends
    neither.

    The cancel tool with no task id, with two running tasks of this
    parent session id, ends both and reports 2, and each of their results is
    the
    stop notice.

    When the newest recorded parent session id of the server's Claude Code
    process changes from A to B, after /clear, the task of A that was still
    running ends as cancelled.

    In a non-interactive Claude Code session that advertises MCP task support,
    a delegate call that does not set wait still waits and returns the
    result, because the task mode is off there.
  }

  interface {
    Let the *delegate tool*, mcp__plugin_subzero_subzero__delegate, accept a
    call with an optional agent name, an optional model tier written as medium
    or tier:medium, a brief text, optional files, an optional expected output
    and an optional wait that defaults to true while the task mode is off and
    to
    false while the task mode is on. Return the result with the task id first.

    Record the parent session id of each conversation through the
    SessionStart hook at each session start.

    Describe the delegate tool with each routable tier and the child host and
    model
    it
    runs,
    read again from the agent file at each tool listing.

    Let the tasks tool list every task of the current parent session id, or
    return
    one task by its task id. Bound a listing to 50 tasks and say how many it
    left
    out.

    Let the *await tool* collect the result of tasks started without waiting,
    each whole or as a deferred line, and let the cancel tool end a task as
    cancelled.

    Give each delegation of the delegate tool the reply budget, so that the
    child knows the size its final reply should stay under.

    Report each task that has no result by its status line in the replies of
    delegate, tasks and await, so that a queued task is never reported as
    started.

    Keep a readiness marker in the plugin data folder for each project while
    the
    server runs, holding its process id and the process id of its parent.

    Be registered with Kernel as the parent adapter of the Claude Code
    parent host by the
    entry point of its process, which is outside ClaudeMcpServer and which also
    imports
    and registers the child drivers that process needs.

    Answer a delegate call that does not set wait to true with an MCP task
    while
    the
    task mode is on, and give its completed MCP task the completion notice.

    Let the cancel tool run a session cancel for the current parent session id
    when it names no task id.

    Accept each tier word, tier name and agent alias that AgentConfig maps, in
    the delegate tool.

    Assume an allow rule from the user or project for a delegation from a
    subagent,
    because the plugin cannot ship one.

    Send a progress notification to a caller that asked for progress, at least
    once
    every 30 seconds while a delegation is running.
  }

  decisions {
    If a platform check shows that MCP tasks do not work in a Claude Code
    session, the planned fallback is a hook that wakes Claude when a child
    finishes, and the contract of ClaudeRoutingHooks is revised then, because
    its hooks read only the agent file and the readiness marker today.

    Only delegate and await are always loaded, so that a parent model can
    dispatch
    a
    batch and collect it with no tool search hop, while the other tools stay
    out
    of the parent model context. Progress
    carries one line from the latest event so that the parent model sees real
    activity
    without receiving the event log. The allow rule stays an assumption in the
    spike because a plugin cannot ship one: the README and the allow-list of
    each
    delegating experiment arm supply it, and a setup command is left to a later
    version.

    A queued task is reported as not launched yet because compound-engineering
    starts each reviewer's 20-minute clock at its launch, and a reviewer late
    in
    the queue of a one-GPU model endpoint would otherwise time out before it
    ran. Queued is derived and not stored because the start-up sweep, the
    listing and the command line all key on the task status running. The
    status line says this session's queue because the queue place is counted
    per process.

    A result is never cut, because a cut result made whether the parent model
    got a
    whole answer depend on how long the child wrote, and cut JSON did not
    parse. Claude Code saves a reply over its inline limit to a file instead of
    truncating it, so the first section of an await reply is always whole and
    the rest are deferred, not cut. The tool result limit is that inline limit
    and the reply budget sits under it, so a child that keeps to it is shown
    inline. This replaces the equal share of a smaller limit that each task in
    an await reply once had.
  }
}
